# From Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework-8 to Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs): Representative Substance for the General Study of Pioneering MOF Applications

## 1. Introduction
## 1.1. Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a subclass of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with isomorphic topologies of zeolites and coordinative composition of MOFs. They are fabricated via the self-assembly of transition metal ions and imidazolate linkers through tetrahedral coordination. Owning an approximate metal-imidazole-metal angle of $145^{\circ}$ and analogous structures to aluminosilicate zeolites, ZIFs possess properties of both MOFs and zeolites, exhibiting combined advantages of both materials.[1] The strong and long bonding among the metal centers and the imidazole centers allows ZIFs to generally have higher thermal and moisture stability than other MOFs[2] and larger cavities than corresponding zeolites[3,4]; therefore, the unique and exceptional properties and applications of ZIFs are under intense research, of which ZIF-8 is the most classic and hottest member.
## 1.2. Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework-8
Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) is a microporous material with a network formula of $\mathrm{Zn}(2\mathrm{-methylimidazole})_{2}$ , briefly written as $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mIm})_{2}\mathrm{^{[5]}}$ Its sodalite topology has a space group of interconnected sixmembered ring windows with an accessible diameter of $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ and pore diameter of $1.16~\mathrm{nm}$ . As a maturely developed MOF that has already been commercialized, present utilizations of ZIF-8 are rather focused on its functions as templates for the study of other materials, components of functional composites, and as precursor for various derivatives than its original functions when working homogeneously. As a highly crystalline porous material, ZIF-8 has been widely used for the study of many novel systems in requirement of MOF structures. With extremely high thermal, chemical, and mechanical stability (ZIF-8 is stable against ammonia from room temperature to $350~^{\circ}C^{[6]}$ and can maintain its porosity and long range order up to ca. $2\mathrm{\GPa^{[7]}}$ ), excellent experimental and molecular simulated adsorption behavior of gases like $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , and, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .[8] ZIF-8 is the perfect representative for the study of multiple methodology toward the design of various porous coordination polymers and has provided us with an enormous number of future uses for industrial purposes.[9,10]
Novel synthetic approaches for different morphologies of purephased ZIF-8 are intensely studied over the years, which provide inspiration for other porous materials. Generally, recent research has shown us many important aspects such as synthesis by ultrasounds,[11] as compared to the conventional solvothermal synthesis.[2] Also, use of different solvents[12] as well as solventless synthesis like mechanosynthesis,[13] high pressure synthesis,[14] and steam assisted synthesis.[15] Typically, generating hierarchical ZIF-8 using
CTAB and amino acid histidine as surfactant directors under ambient aqueous conditions is reported.[16] During the co-templating process, histidine was chosen to be the chelaters for Zn(II) to gain the stable interaction between metal ions and micelles. First, CTAB and citric acid cooperatively form the HKUST-1 framework. As the amino acid prechelates the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions, it plays a significant role in stabilizing the CTAB micelles and in preventing the formation of undesired by-products, thus promoting the construction of pure-phased ZIF-8, meaning the ZIF-8 crystallinity can be enhanced by controlling pore sizes on the microand mesoscale. The templating strategies of MOF nanorods, nanotubes, and nanowires have also been respectively demonstrated on ZIF-8.[17] For the modification of MOFs toward better performances in applications, ZIF-8 is also a universal demonstrator. For example, a low-cost and facile thermal annealing of ZIF-8 result in an optimized structure featuring partly dissociation of methyl groups on the ligands and partial break of the coordination bonds, thus bringing huge enhancement in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake as well as in CO2/N $2$ selectivity to the framework.[18] This thermal treatment is simpler and more effective, compared to other post-synthesis chemical modifications. An anisotropic wet-chemical post-etching strategy was devised to obtain hollow sodalite ZIF-8 crystals.[19] Hybridized derivatives in the form of hollow nanocrystals can also be obtained from ZIF-8 and molecules like tannic acid by post synthetic modification.[20] Similarly, an interfacial microfluidic processing of ZIF-8 hollow fiber membranes can also be achieved.[21]
## 2. Applications of ZIF-8 Based Composites, Derivatives, and Systems
## 2.1. Adsorption and Separation
## 2.1.1. Adsorption and Separation of Small Molecules
Permeability and highly tunable physical and chemical properties render ZIF-8 suitable for many possibilities of specific separation of every conceivable mixture,[22] for example, biomimetic superhydrophobic or superoleophilic fluorinated graphene oxide@ZIF-8 composites for oil– water separation.[23] On account of the easy chemistry and exceptional tunability of ZIF-8 framework,[24] many different approaches toward pure-phased ZIF-8 membranes can be utilized to obtain diverse properties (Table 1), apart from which ZIF-8 membranes synthesized on inorganic polymeric hollow fibers pose potential commercialization for gas separation. For instance, the synthesis of ZIF-8 on Torlon,[21] polysulfone,[25] polyimide P84,[26] PVDF,[27] and even on metal hollow fiber supports.[28] Moreover, when combined with other materials, not only the functions of ZIF-8 and the other participants add up together, the interaction of the components may eliminate the disadvantages of ZIF-8 or those of its coagent. Typically, the fabrication of polymer/ZIF-8 hybrid membrane can well solve the technical boundaries of membrane which often arise from aspects like mechanical stability, trade-off between separation and permeability, and reproducibility. To which a water-based, drying-free process to disperse ZIF-8 particles in polymer matrix is devised to fabricate membranes composed of a mixed matrix of ZIF-8 and hydrophilic organic polymers, thus producing poly (vinyl alcohol)/ZIF-8 membranes with outstanding pervaporation dehydration performance.[29]
A recently modified chemical approach to ZIF-8 films attached on nylon substrate is contra-diffusion,[30] which poses new scope for the film formation of MOFs. The biomineralization based ZIF-8, specifically, the chemical attachment of ZIF-8 layers onto diatom frustules was
