## 2.1.2. Adsorption of Organic Compound
In the same sense of gas adsorption, ZIF-8 is highly useful when applied to adsorb and release organic compounds for environmental protection or drug release,[38] especially when it cooperates with other functional materials that serve different stages of the composite’s working process. Most recently, a Janus composite, Au@ZnO@ZIF-8 for simultaneous adsorption and formaldehyde removal under mild temperature.[39] In addition, for ZIF-8 derivatives, they can be thermally optimized for enhancement in gas uptakes.[18] Particularly, an organophilic pervaporation membrane derived from ZIF-8 nanoparticles exhibits efficient recovery of bio-alcohols.[40]
First-principle calculation and molecular simulation of the dynamics and energetics of drug molecules in MOFs has been intensively studied.[41] While directly administrating the therapeutic agents suffer a lack of control over drug molecule diffusion, MOFs with tailorable chemistry, tunable and uniform cavities, as well as high porosity can serve as the perfect candidates for the incorporation of molecules with distinct shapes and sizes.[42] For instance, hierarchical ZIF-8 synthesized via the one-pot method can be encapsulated with large drug molecules for anti-cancer drug doxorubicins used in cancer therapy. By tuning the responsive pH release, the loading capacity of the composite can be functional materials. Typically, the integration of metal particles inside mSiO2@ZIF-8 (mSiO2 = mesoporous silica) can result in spherical monodispersed metal-mSiO $_2(\stackrel{}{\underbrace{a}})$ ZIF-8 core-shell composite as extraordinary nanocatalysts.[50] Notably, while most of the catalysts examples given correspond to ZIF-8 containing catalysts, ZIF-8 due to the presence of Zn metal has certain innate catalytic activity.[51]
Not only high electroactivity can be achieved on derivatives from a single ZIF-8, ZIF-8 hybridized with other ZIFs with matched topology brings new insights into the derivations with improved properties,[52] such works have been conducted on ZIF-7,[53] ZIF-9,[54] ZIF-90,[55] and ZIF-67.[56] A hierarchical carbon nanopolyhedra (Co, N-HCNP) was synthesized by codoping cobalt and nitrogen via the carbonization of core-shell structured ZIF-8 ${\mathfrak{a}}$ ZIF-67 crystals. The product possesses considerable ORR activity and wields a kinetic current density decided as $63.84~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in accordance with the KouteckyLevich (K–L) equation. Along with a half-wave potential of $855~\mathrm{mV}$ and an electron transfer number of 3.71, all of which suggest the outstanding electrocatalytic ORR activity of Co, N-HCNP.[57]
