## 2.2. Catalysis
Ni/ZIF-8 nanocatalyst presents excellent efficiency in catalyzing the hydrolysis of ammonia borane.[58] Nanoparticles were well incorporated into the cages of ZIF-8 via deposition–precipitation so that they can be confined and stabilized, bringing controlled nucleation and growth, which renders Ni/ZIF-8 nanocatalyst to overperform even some pure-phased noble metal nanoparticles. Notably, analysis (especially the kinetic isotope effect) suggests that the reaction rate is depended on cleavage by the addition of oxidative O–H into $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Therefore, an “on–off” control over hydrogen revolution by merely controllably adding $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ is
The most reported use of ZIF-8 as catalysts is to use it to accommodate catalytically active particles, the controllable porous framework of ZIF-8 can act as molecule separator for selective catalysis, also the ordered structure promotes mass diffusion.[47] For example, the modified coreshell structure of Pd@ZIF-8 for high-efficiency photocatalysis, during which the ligands of ZIF-8 reach out to absorb more energy from light.[48] The initially microporous structure of ZIF-8 provides highdensity active sites for various catalysis. The exceptionally tunable framework of ZIF-8 also enables people to adjust its porosity to a mesoporous or hierarchical degree in many ways,[49] which makes it possible that ZIF-8 can cooperate in catalytic processes with many other achieved. The resulting Ni/ZIF-8 holds a turn-over frequency reaching up to $85.7\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{H_{2}}}\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , indicative of a promising future for chemically storing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in fuel cell bases.
Innately microporous ZIF-8 can be granted with hierarchy by sizeselective catalysis and local transformation.[59] The resulting ZIF-8 nanocrystal exhibited greatly enhanced catalysis of the esterification of oleic acid with glycerol even at low temperatures.[60] By embedding catalase in ZIF-8 pores, cruciate flower-like composites denoted as catalase $@$ ZIF-8 can be obtained (Figure 4). The catalytic activity of the composites is $40\%$ higher than that of the composites with a rhombic dodecahedral morphology.[61]
## 2.3. Electrode for Batteries
The carbonization of ZIF-8 via pyrolysis has led to many derivatives that possess one common advantage that most pure-phased MOFs do not have electroconductivity. $\mathrm{LiFePO_{4}}$ (LFP) has been widely reported recently as a cathode composer for lithium-ion batteries. In situ growth of ZIF-8 and following carbonization resulted in an $\mathrm{LFP/C_{ZIF-8}}$ that possesses improved conductivity resulted from a $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick carbon coating with high concentration of N doping because of the original organic ligand in ZIF-8. Additionally, the porous derivative promotes lithium-ion diffusion, giving rise to a lithium electrode that outperforms previous versions.[62]
Similar process of introducing ZIF-8 into electrode can be done backwards for different properties. Via magnesiothermic reduction reaction (MRR), ZnO/MgO coated by mesoporous silicon can be obtained by using ZIF-8 coated by SiO $2$ as the sacrificial template (Figure 5). $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was first deposited onto the surface of ZIF-8, then calcinated, then treated by MRR, and finally, followed by an etching process. Due to its considerably improved conductivity, efficiency, and stability, the resulting m-Si HC can be used as anodes for lithium-ion batteries.[63] Analogously, monodispersed mesoporous carbon nanospheres with porphyrin-like zinc centers (PMCS) modified by PEG–vitamin E can be obtained the carbonization of SiO $2$ -coated ZIF-8 and a following etching treatment.[64]
As for pure-phased ZIF-8 derivatives, nanoporous carbons (NPCs) pose new scopes for MOF electrodes.[65] Typically, studies on the synthesis of small- and large-sized ZIF-8 and their corresponding carbonized derivatives. Observed from the SEM and TEM images, the NPCs are polyhedrally shaped (Figure 6). The capacitance value of the NPCs derived from large-sized ZIF-8 was analyzed by standard twoelectrode and three-electrode measurements, reaching $252\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}$ while that of the ones derived from ZIF-8 of small sizes is $125\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}$ , showing that the control over MOFs size has effects on the electrochemical properties.
## 2.4. Role as Modeling Framework for Novel Studies of General MOFs
Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 is the most frequently selected sample to perform newly developed synthetic approaches of MOF-based functional systems. The high tunability, stability, and accessibility of ZIF-8 allow researchers to conduct a great many exploration in the general field of applicable porous materials and their characterization methods. For example, very recently, the self-assembly of super-structured ZIF-8 is studied.[66] From a physically controlling perspective, ZIF-8 shows how electric fields constrain the mobility of the organic ligands to reduce the flexibility of the crystal structure, making it more solid, and the kinetical change of the pore sizes decreases and strengthens the separation efficiency. The resulting topological transition of ZIF-8 shows higher performances in a targeted separation.[67,68]
Traditionally, the assembly of ZIF-8 nanoparticles to the formation of bulk structures to promote gas uptake is an alternative way to modify the performances of MOFs. Further study has conducted the in situ attachment of MOFs on functionalized materials.[69] Novel properties may be offered by the fabrication of MOF nanoparticles. However, the surface area of bulk ZIF-8 is drastically outmatched by that of ZIF-8 nanoparticles (ca. $800{\mathrm{-}}1200{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}.$ ) presumably due to incomplete activation or the amorphous compounds. What is indicated by the simulated calculations is that the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption capacity over bulk ZIF-8 exceeds that over the nanoparticles, particularly under low pressures because bulk MOFs have more neighboring atoms inside. Thus, assembling MOF nanoparticles into bulky forms is vital for retaining an ideal uptake of gases. Composites containing MOF nanoparticles and functionalized materials may be the solution, while bringing promoted processability at the nanoscale. For instance, amino-clay was used as an amine-functionalized supporter to template the growth of ZIF
8 nanoparticles (Figure 7), featuring a reaction after the direct mixture of ZIF-8 precursors and amino-clay in aqueous conditions. The chemically stable coordination bonds between ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and amine groups resulted in bulky ZIF-8 that exhibits enhanced uptake of gas adsorption. Interestingly, by controlling the extent of adding amino-clay during the reaction, the gate pressure can be judiciously adjusted because of the tunable surface area of ZIF-8. Additionally, in situ Raman detection exhibits that CO $\angle$ adsorption over the composites could be further facilitated by bonding sites provided by amine groups to fix the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. Such composite also portrays promising potentials in light harvesting or electrocatalysis.
While ZIF-8 membranes can be synthesized by many methods for diverse purposes, $\lfloor70-74\rfloor$ as for MOFs films, ZIF-8 is utilized to show the tunable thickness of MOF thin films improved adsorption properties,[32] contra-diffusion on a porous nylon substrate for ZIF-8 films synthesis,[30] as well as MOF layer covering ultra-hydrophilic graphene to form Li-S battery after being thermally exfoliated. $\lfloor75\rfloor$ Further studies of these applications need more abbreviated fabrication of MOF films. Obviously, bulk MOFs are outperformed by robust MOF thin films in fields like conductors, microelectronics, and sensor coating. However, most reported depositions of MOF thin films are conducted by aligning MOF particles synthesized under conditions similar to conventional solvothermal processes. The vast incompatibility between these approaches and the fabrication of microelectronic devices may result in devastating synthesizing environment due to the inevitable contact with the synthetic solution that is responsible for chemical contamination and corrosion toward the microelectronic circuitry. Furthermore, the homogeneous nucleation of MOF particles in aqueous systems poses extra contamination. The third drawback is that the cost of solvents can be so high that it compromises the basic requirement of green fabrication, which is mandatory in the industry of microelectronics. To solve the above issues, as well as to delicately fabricate MOF thin films of high uniformity that possess controlled thickness, one strategy named chemical vapor deposition (CVD) perfectly integrates MOFs with microelectronics,[76] even allowing for originally inaccessible methods such as the lift-off strategy of MOF coating on devices and depositing MOFs films onto fragile features. While in this case, microporous ZIF-8 served as the demonstrator, this MOFCVD method poses greater potential in fabricating MOF films because of the higher synthetic precision required by the low packing density of mesoporosity, otherwise the disadvantages of mesoporous surface which can be easily obtained by conventional fabrication can completely outweigh their advantages as novel optical or electro devices.
Typically, PVA/ZIF-8 mixed matrix membrane (MMM) was first reported in 2010.[77] PVA/ZIF-8 is successfully fabricated to obtain MMMs to demonstrate a new method to make MOF-based high-performance membranes (Scheme 1).[29] Other combinations with polysulfone,[78] polyimides,[79] pebax,[80] PBI[81] and so on have also been conducted. Notably, PBI MMMs also demonstrate that when it comes to particle engineering, the particle size of ZIF-8 as filler may influence on the MMM performance.[82]
While the integrations of MOFs with NPs primarily focus on design and performance, for example, the encapsulation of 12-tungstophosphoric heteropolyacid (HPW) into the pores of MIL-100(Fe), with the resulting composite denoted as HPW@MIL-100(Fe), which can function as the heterogeneous catalyst for esterification and acetalization.[83] Few researches notice the precise control over the distribution and diffusion of nanoparticles in MOFs. Due to the molecular sieving effects resulted from the porous character of MOFs, metal nanoparticles@MOF (MNPs@MOF), such as the yolk–shell structure based on ZIF-8, exhibits promising catalytic activities.[84] However, the molecular diffusion rate has been kept at a low level due to the narrow MOF channels, which would bring about poor reaction rate. Interestingly, ZIF-8 can be encapsulated with molecules like caffeine[85] and even ones with larger sizes that the ZIF-8 openings, such “gate opening” effect has been reported in consequence of the ZIF-8 structurally flexibility.[86] In a typical system featuring the encapsulation of MNPs in MOFs, the catalytic activity rises with the closer distribution of MNPs to the MOF surface.[87] This study also discovers the mechanism of the controlled distribution of NPs by adjusting the time of reaction time (Figure 8a,b) or the organic ligand concentration, (Figure 8c–h) during which the metal oxides carrying NPs were loaded on its surface as a sacrificial template. Such controlled immobilization of MNPs into MOFs indicate that the regulation of composites based on MOFs and nanoparticles can be conducted by varying the nucleation conditions for MOFs, which significantly prompts the field of incorporating NPs incorporation in porous materials for greater catalytic performance forward. This study also provides inspiration to the understanding of the mechanism behind NP diffusion in MOFs with meso-sized cavities, further offering potentials for the encapsulation of organic parts in MOFs that relates uses in biomedicine, as well as the encapsulation of nanoparticles for selective sensing and catalysis,[88] while ZIF-8 can be further devised in devices for the selective sensing of chemical vapors and gases.[89]
When serving as molecule-size selector in an adsorptive or catalytic system, ZIF-8 also brings other properties to the composite. For example, the surface growth of ZIF-8 crystals on carbon nitride foam results in a multifunctional hierarchical micro- and mesoporous structure that can selectively absorb oil spills from water and efficiently convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into cyclic carbonates. Notably, the hydrophobic nature of ZIF-8 protects the foam from water instability, which can be a general strategy for the use of certain MOF composites.[10] Moreover, under certain circumstances like a double solvent condition, controlled encapsulation of functional organic molecules inside ZIF-8 with a leaf-like morphology can be achieved by in situ crystalline structure transformation,[90] the resulting composite exhibits highly efficient selectivity in fluorescence sensing. Also, double-solvent mediation can lead to the overgrowth of microporous ZIF-8 can offer mesoporously hollow structure inside the single nanocrystals (Scheme 2).[91]
Other than the preparation of encapsulation or surface coating of ZIF-8 discussed above, the direct control over the porosity over pure-phased MOFs like ZIF-8 nanocrystals[39,92] is more fundamentally
Mesoporous and macroporous MOFs outperform microporous MOFs in the adsorption, separation, catalysis, drug delivery and controlled release of large molecules such as complex organic compounds, proteins, enzymes, DNAs, and so on, and a series of synthetic routes have been introduced to successfully fabricate MOFs with large pores above the microporous scope $(>2\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). However, these MOFs are either structurally disordered or suffer from fragile and easily collapsed crystallinity because of their low packing density. To solve this pickle, an ingenious double-solvent method has been performed to achieve a highly ordered singlecrystal ZIF-8 structure.[93] The initially microporous nature and high tunability and synthetic accessibility of ZIF-8 allow the fine crystallization following the immersion of ZIF-8 precursor in between the macropores provided by a threedimensionally structured PS (polystyrene sphere) particles (Figure 9). The resulting ordered macro-micro single crystal possesses a catalytic activity over four times higher than that of pure phase microporous ZIF-8 in terms of the Knoevenagel reaction between benzaldehydes and malononitriles. What’s more, it is observed that the catalytic activity obviously rises with the increasing sizes of reactants. The remarkable stability of the macroporous ZIF-8 framework can be proved by its over $87\%$ maintenance of the initial activity after seven catalytic cycles. The universality of this strategy lies in the indication that the diameters of the ordered macropores $(190-470\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) can be systematically and judiciously controlled by changing the size of the PS templates.
